\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

To contextualize depth discrimination ability rather than claim competitive advantage over detection-optimized methods, we repurpose four detection scores as ordinal depth predictors using the same XGBoost framework and LOGO splits (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, Figure~\ref{fig:baseline-ci}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.pdf}
\caption{LOGO binary F1 (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+, 8-gen pooled) with inference cost. Ensembles achieve marginally higher F1 at 2.4$\times$ inference time and 3.2$\times$ GPU memory; individual detectors vary widely when repurposed for depth.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Feat.} & \textbf{3-cl F1} & \textbf{Bin F1} & \textbf{ms/text} \\
\midrule
4-det.\ + surface     & 6 & \textbf{0.795} & \textbf{0.708} & 22.8 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 4 & 0.785 & 0.700 & 22.8 \\
7-feature (ours)      & 7 & 0.774 & 0.705 & \textbf{9.7} \\
Avg-LogProb           & 1 & 0.707 & 0.673 & N/A \\
FastDetectGPT         & 1 & 0.660 & 0.569 & N/A \\
LD-Score              & 1 & 0.630 & 0.675 & N/A \\
Binoculars            & 1 & 0.411 & 0.539 & N/A \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification (8-gen LOGO, pooled evaluation; slightly higher than per-generator macro average in Table~\ref{tab:logo-results}). Identical XGBoost configuration. 7-feature: one 410M model (905\,MB); 4-det ensembles: two models, 1.4B params (2{,}868\,MB).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble exceeds all individual baselines in 3-class F1 ($\Delta$\,=\,6.7--36.3\,pp).
Multi-detector ensembles achieve higher 3-class F1 (0.795 vs.\ 0.774) and marginally higher binary F1 (0.708 vs.\ 0.705) at 2.4$\times$ inference cost and 3.2$\times$ GPU memory; our contribution is the DDR analysis and task formulation, not classifier performance.
Preliminary downstream evaluation finds that depth composition does not determine training utility at fixed sample count (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).

\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{TOST equivalence testing across multiple margins. ``Equiv?'' indicates whether equivalence is established at $\alpha = 0.05$.}
\label{tab:tost-margins}
\end{table}

The full dataset vs.\ random subsample comparison passes equivalence at the $\pm$2\% margin ($p = 10^{-6}$), while the full vs.\ depth 0--1 comparison requires a $\pm$3\% margin.
In absolute terms, perplexity differences are small: $\Delta$\,=\,0.032 (full vs.\ random, 1.4\% of baseline) and $\Delta$\,=\,0.052 (full vs.\ depth 0--1, 2.2\% of baseline).

Cohen's $d$ values are 86.0 (full vs.\ random) and 119.7 (full vs.\ depth 0--1).
These appear large but are unreliable: with only $n$\,=\,3 seeds per condition, the pooled standard deviation is extremely small ($<$0.001), artificially inflating $d$.
The inflated effect sizes reflect low seed-level variance (confirming reproducibility) rather than practically meaningful perplexity differences.
The TOST framework with pre-specified relative margins provides a more appropriate assessment than Cohen's $d$ in this low-seed regime.

